//! member: true
//!
//! std.x.jose — the JOSE envelope ACME needs, over sc18's Ed25519:
//! JWS signing/verification with EdDSA (RFC 7515 + RFC 8037), the
//! Ed25519 public JWK and its RFC 7638 thumbprint (RFC 7517 + 7638),
//! and the ACME request envelope (RFC 8555 §6.2). Stability tier: x —
//! the D53 crypto ladder's FOURTH rung (campaign stdc-crypto, sprint
//! sc19). This rung is PLUMBING, not new crypto: every signature is
//! sc18's `std.x.crypto.curve25519.sign`/`verify`, every digest is
//! sc16's `std.x.crypto.sha2.sum256`, and the base64url is sc05's
//! `std.base64.url_encode`/`url_decode`. The human chose ACME over
//! AES-GCM breadth: the pinned local test CA speaks Ed25519, so there
//! is NO P-256 here (that stays a named D-question for the breadth
//! rung).
//!
//! **The nursery banner (D31).** `std.x.jose` is not `std.jose`: its
//! path, names and behaviour may change or vanish in one sprint; no
//! other std module may depend on it. Its reason and graduation trigger
//! live in `std/x/README.md`. It depends on TWO sibling residents —
//! `std.x.crypto.curve25519` (the signature) and `std.x.crypto.sha2`
//! (the thumbprint digest) — plus the facade (`std.base64`, `std.bytes`,
//! `std.str`); a resident MAY depend on the facade, and the two
//! resident-on-resident edges are ones the graduation move to `std.jose`
//! /`std.crypto` must resolve, named in the register beside curve25519's
//! own sha2 edge.
//!
//! ## The three byte-exact rules this module lives or dies on
//!
//! 1. **base64url is NOT base64** (RFC 4648 §5, not §4): the alphabet
//!    ends `-_`, never `+/`, and JOSE carries NO padding. A base64
//!    -standard reuse silently corrupts every signature and every
//!    thumbprint, because the signing input and the hash input are the
//!    *encoded* text. This module calls `std.base64.url_encode` (no pad)
//!    and `std.base64.url_decode` (padding tolerated on the way in, the
//!    §5 sentence) EXCLUSIVELY; `encode`/`decode` (the §4 padded pair)
//!    never appear. The negatives in `tests/x/jose/base64url_jose.lu`
//!    pin the alphabet and the no-pad rule.
//!
//! 2. **The signing input is over the BASE64URL STRINGS, not the raw
//!    bytes** (RFC 7515 §5.1): the message Ed25519 signs is the ASCII
//!    octets of `BASE64URL(protected) || '.' || BASE64URL(payload)`.
//!    The classic JWS bug is signing the decoded payload; `signing_input`
//!    builds the encoded form and `jws_sign_compact` signs *its* bytes.
//!    RFC 8037 A.4's vector — signature `hgyY0il_…M0KAg` over the
//!    signing input `eyJhbGciOiJFZERTQSJ9.RXhhbXBsZSBvZiBFZDI1NTE5IHNpZ25pbmc`
//!    — is the pin.
//!
//! 3. **The JWK thumbprint is byte-exact, canonical, no-whitespace**
//!    (RFC 7638 §3): SHA-256 over the required members in LEXICOGRAPHIC
//!    order with no spaces and no newlines. For an OKP Ed25519 key the
//!    required members are `crv`, `kty`, `x`, in that order, so the hash
//!    input is exactly `{"crv":"Ed25519","kty":"OKP","x":"<x>"}`. This
//!    module BUILDS that string directly (string interpolation) rather
//!    than routing through `std.json`'s writer, for two measured reasons:
//!    (a) `std.json` carries an enum and a `Map` that darken the compiler
//!    lanes (F-0012/F-0029), and the thumbprint must stay on every lane
//!    the digest reaches; (b) the three OKP member VALUES are a fixed
//!    ASCII pair (`Ed25519`, `OKP`) and a base64url string, none of
//!    which contains a character JSON escapes, so direct construction is
//!    byte-identical to what any conforming writer emits. RFC 7638's own
//!    RSA example and RFC 8037 A.3's OKP thumbprint
//!    (`kPrK_qmxVWaYVA9wwBF6Iuo3vVzz7TxHCTwXBygrS4k`) are the pins.
//!
//! ## EdDSA is deterministic; the ACME `nonce` is a different thing
//! Ed25519 signing (RFC 8032 §5.1.6, sc18) derives its per-signature
//! nonce from the key and message by hashing — there is NO entropy input
//! and NO randomness on this module's path. The ACME `nonce` header
//! field (RFC 8555 §6.4) is an anti-replay token the SERVER issues and
//! the client echoes; it is protocol state, not signature entropy. The
//! two never touch. This module has no entropy story and needs none.
//!
//! ## ct: posture
//! Signing touches the secret account key, so `jws_sign_compact`,
//! `jws_flattened` and `acme_body` inherit sc18's `curve25519.sign` ct
//! path verbatim (the recorded scalarmul-bit-gate and `floordiv`
//! sign-correction obligations c28 verifies; no NEW secret handling is
//! added here — the key flows straight into `sign`). Everything else is
//! PUBLIC-DATA framing: the protected header, the payload, the JWK, the
//! thumbprint and every base64url string are public by construction (a
//! JWS is transmitted in the clear), and `jws_verify_compact` is public
//! on all three operands exactly as `curve25519.verify` is. So the ct
//! surface of this rung is precisely sc18's signing surface and nothing
//! more; the JOSE framing carries no timing obligation.
//!
//! ## THE ws06 SEAM (RFC 8555 §6.2) — FROZEN for the `cert auto` track
//! This sprint builds the ENVELOPE and its serialization, NOT the ACME
//! protocol state machine (newNonce/newAccount/newOrder/challenge/
//! finalize over the network is wws's ws06). The seam ws06 consumes is
//! exactly this, and it does not move:
//!
//!   - ws06 OWNS: the HTTPS conversation, fetching `Replay-Nonce`, the
//!     directory, the order/authorization/challenge/finalize flow, and
//!     the account-key lifecycle.
//!   - sc19 OWNS: given a secret key and the header fields, produce the
//!     signed request BODY. Two calls:
//!       1. build the protected header — `acme_protected_jwk(pubkey,
//!          nonce, url)` for the newAccount request (the key is inlined
//!          as a `jwk`), or `acme_protected_kid(kid, nonce, url)` for
//!          every request thereafter (the account URL is the `kid`);
//!       2. sign and serialize — `acme_body(secret, protected, payload)`
//!          returns the flattened-JWS JSON `{"protected":…,"payload":…,
//!          "signature":…}` that is the POST body. A POST-as-GET carries
//!          an EMPTY payload (`List[int]()`), whose base64url is `""`,
//!          per RFC 8555 §6.3.
//!   - The account binding uses `jwk_thumbprint(pubkey)` (RFC 7638),
//!     which ws06 places wherever the server's challenge demands it.
//!
//! ws06 supplies bytes and strings; sc19 returns a spec-shaped body.
//! Neither reaches into the other. The fixture in
//! `tests/x/jose/acme_envelope.lu` is the frozen shape ws06 asserts
//! against.

use std.base64
use std.bytes
use std.str
use std.x.crypto.curve25519
use std.x.crypto.sha2

/// The hex DIGIT (lowercase) for a nibble 0..15. Private, for `esc`'s
/// `\u00XX` control-character escapes.
fn hexdigit(d: int) -> int {
    if d < 10 {
        48 + d
    } else {
        87 + d
    }
}

/// JSON string-body escaping (RFC 8259 §7): the two mandatory
/// characters (`"` and `\`), the five short control forms, and `\u00XX`
/// for every other C0 control. Returns the escaped body WITHOUT the
/// surrounding quotes. Private.
///
/// Total: it walks the input's bytes and rebuilds through
/// `std.bytes.to_str`; every inserted byte is ASCII and every passed
/// byte came from a valid `str`, so the result is always valid UTF-8 and
/// the `utf8` row never fires (the `else` is unreachable, defaulted to
/// the empty string rather than asserted so a caller cannot be trapped
/// by a framing helper).
///
/// The ACME header fields this escapes — `nonce`, `url`, `kid` — are a
/// base64url token and two https URLs in practice, none carrying a
/// character that escapes; the escaper is here so a hostile or unusual
/// field cannot break out of its JSON string, which is what "defensive"
/// means for an envelope other people's servers fill in.
fn esc(s: str) -> str {
    var out = List[int]()
    for b in s.bytes() {
        if b == 34 {
            (mut out).push(92)
            (mut out).push(34)
        } else if b == 92 {
            (mut out).push(92)
            (mut out).push(92)
        } else if b == 8 {
            (mut out).push(92)
            (mut out).push(98)
        } else if b == 9 {
            (mut out).push(92)
            (mut out).push(116)
        } else if b == 10 {
            (mut out).push(92)
            (mut out).push(110)
        } else if b == 12 {
            (mut out).push(92)
            (mut out).push(102)
        } else if b == 13 {
            (mut out).push(92)
            (mut out).push(114)
        } else if b < 32 {
            (mut out).push(92)
            (mut out).push(117)
            (mut out).push(48)
            (mut out).push(48)
            (mut out).push(hexdigit(b / 16))
            (mut out).push(hexdigit(b % 16))
        } else {
            (mut out).push(b)
        }
    }
    bytes.to_str(out) else ""
}

/// The JWS Signing Input (RFC 7515 §5.1): `BASE64URL(UTF8(protected)) ||
/// '.' || BASE64URL(payload)`. This is the octet string Ed25519 signs —
/// over the ENCODED text, never the raw bytes (§2's classic-bug note).
/// `protected` is the header JSON (its UTF-8 is encoded); `payload` is
/// already a byte sequence and is encoded as-is.
///
/// ```wolf-doc-example
/// let payload = "Example of Ed25519 signing".bytes()
/// jose.signing_input("{{\"alg\":\"EdDSA\"}}", payload) == "eyJhbGciOiJFZERTQSJ9.RXhhbXBsZSBvZiBFZDI1NTE5IHNpZ25pbmc"
/// ```
pub fn signing_input(protected: str, payload: List[int]) -> str {
    let p = base64.url_encode(protected.bytes())
    let pl = base64.url_encode(payload)
    "{p}.{pl}"
}

/// A JWS in Compact Serialization (RFC 7515 §7.1) with EdDSA (RFC 8037):
/// `BASE64URL(protected) || '.' || BASE64URL(payload) || '.' ||
/// BASE64URL(signature)`, the signature being Ed25519 over the signing
/// input's ASCII bytes. `secret` is the 32-byte Ed25519 secret key;
/// `protected` is the protected-header JSON (for a plain EdDSA JWS,
/// `{"alg":"EdDSA"}`); `payload` is the message bytes.
///
/// Deterministic: the same key, header and payload always yield the same
/// compact string (Ed25519 has no nonce input). Traps only where
/// `curve25519.sign` does (a secret key that is not 32 bytes is a caller
/// contract violation there).
///
/// ```wolf-doc-example
/// let sk = "a 32-byte ed25519 secret key !!!".bytes()
/// let jws = jose.jws_sign_compact(sk, "{{\"alg\":\"EdDSA\"}}", "hi".bytes())
/// jws.starts_with("eyJhbGciOiJFZERTQSJ9.aGk.")
/// ```
pub fn jws_sign_compact(secret: List[int], protected: str, payload: List[int]) -> str {
    let si = signing_input(protected, payload)
    let sig = curve25519.sign(secret, si.bytes())
    let sb = base64.url_encode(sig)
    "{si}.{sb}"
}

/// Verify a compact JWS against an Ed25519 public key. `true` iff the
/// string is three base64url segments joined by `.` whose last segment
/// is a valid Ed25519 signature over the first two joined by `.`.
///
/// Public data on every operand (a JWS travels in the clear), so this is
/// a BOOL and never traps on a bad token — a wrong segment count, a
/// segment that is not base64url, a wrong-length key or signature, and a
/// forged signature all answer `false`, exactly as `curve25519.verify`
/// answers its own rejection cases (§2's rule: verify decides, it does
/// not trap). It does NOT parse the header's `alg`: this module signs
/// and verifies EdDSA-over-Ed25519 ONLY, so there is no algorithm agility
/// to confuse — a token signed under any other algorithm simply fails the
/// Ed25519 check.
///
/// ```wolf-doc-example
/// jose.jws_verify_compact("x".bytes(), "not.a.jws") == false
/// jose.jws_verify_compact("x".bytes(), "onlyonepart") == false
/// ```
pub fn jws_verify_compact(pubkey: List[int], compact: str) -> bool {
    let parts = str.split(compact, ".")
    if parts.len != 3 {
        return false
    }
    let si = "{parts[0]}.{parts[1]}"
    let sig = base64.url_decode(parts[2]) else |_| {
        return false
    }
    curve25519.verify(pubkey, si.bytes(), sig)
}

/// A JWS in Flattened JSON Serialization (RFC 7515 §7.2.2) with EdDSA:
/// the object `{"protected":"…","payload":"…","signature":"…"}`, each
/// value a base64url string. This is the shape ACME's request body takes
/// (RFC 8555 §6.2); `acme_body` is the ACME-named door to it. The three
/// values are base64url and so need no JSON escaping.
///
/// ```wolf-doc-example
/// let sk = "a 32-byte ed25519 secret key !!!".bytes()
/// let flat = jose.jws_flattened(sk, "{{\"alg\":\"EdDSA\"}}", "hi".bytes())
/// flat.starts_with("{{\"protected\":\"eyJhbGciOiJFZERTQSJ9\",\"payload\":\"aGk\",\"signature\":\"")
/// flat.ends_with("\"}}")
/// ```
pub fn jws_flattened(secret: List[int], protected: str, payload: List[int]) -> str {
    let p_b64 = base64.url_encode(protected.bytes())
    let pl_b64 = base64.url_encode(payload)
    let si = "{p_b64}.{pl_b64}"
    let sig = curve25519.sign(secret, si.bytes())
    let sb = base64.url_encode(sig)
    "{{\"protected\":\"{p_b64}\",\"payload\":\"{pl_b64}\",\"signature\":\"{sb}\"}}"
}

/// The Ed25519 public key as an OKP JWK (RFC 8037 §2, RFC 7517):
/// `{"kty":"OKP","crv":"Ed25519","x":"<BASE64URL(pubkey)>"}` — the member
/// order of RFC 8037 A.2's own example. `pubkey` is the 32-byte Ed25519
/// public key (`curve25519.public`'s output). This is the `jwk` ACME
/// inlines in a newAccount request; its member order is free (a JWK is
/// read by name), so it is NOT the canonical thumbprint form — use
/// `jwk_thumbprint_input` for that.
///
/// ```wolf-doc-example
/// let pk = "a 32-byte ed25519 public key !!!".bytes()
/// jose.jwk_ed25519_public(pk) == "{{\"kty\":\"OKP\",\"crv\":\"Ed25519\",\"x\":\"YSAzMi1ieXRlIGVkMjU1MTkgcHVibGljIGtleSAhISE\"}}"
/// ```
pub fn jwk_ed25519_public(pubkey: List[int]) -> str {
    let x = base64.url_encode(pubkey)
    "{{\"kty\":\"OKP\",\"crv\":\"Ed25519\",\"x\":\"{x}\"}}"
}

/// The canonical thumbprint hash input (RFC 7638 §3) for an OKP Ed25519
/// key: the required members `crv`, `kty`, `x` in lexicographic order,
/// no whitespace — `{"crv":"Ed25519","kty":"OKP","x":"<x>"}`. This is
/// exactly the string RFC 8037 A.3 hashes; it is exposed so a test can
/// pin it byte-for-byte, and it is what `jwk_thumbprint` digests.
///
/// ```wolf-doc-example
/// let pk = "a 32-byte ed25519 public key !!!".bytes()
/// jose.jwk_thumbprint_input(pk) == "{{\"crv\":\"Ed25519\",\"kty\":\"OKP\",\"x\":\"YSAzMi1ieXRlIGVkMjU1MTkgcHVibGljIGtleSAhISE\"}}"
/// ```
pub fn jwk_thumbprint_input(pubkey: List[int]) -> str {
    let x = base64.url_encode(pubkey)
    "{{\"crv\":\"Ed25519\",\"kty\":\"OKP\",\"x\":\"{x}\"}}"
}

/// The RFC 7638 JWK Thumbprint of an OKP Ed25519 key: base64url (no pad)
/// of SHA-256 over `jwk_thumbprint_input(pubkey)`. This is the account
/// identifier ACME binds a key to; RFC 8037 A.3's own vector is
/// `kPrK_qmxVWaYVA9wwBF6Iuo3vVzz7TxHCTwXBygrS4k`.
///
/// ```wolf-doc-example
/// let pk = "a 32-byte ed25519 public key !!!".bytes()
/// jose.jwk_thumbprint(pk).len == 43
/// ```
pub fn jwk_thumbprint(pubkey: List[int]) -> str {
    let canon = jwk_thumbprint_input(pubkey)
    let h = sha2.sum256(canon.bytes())
    base64.url_encode(h)
}

/// The ACME protected header for a request that INLINES the account key
/// (RFC 8555 §6.2, the newAccount case): `{"alg":"EdDSA","jwk":<jwk>,
/// "nonce":"<nonce>","url":"<url>"}`. `pubkey` is the 32-byte Ed25519
/// public key; `nonce` is the server's `Replay-Nonce`; `url` is the
/// request target. The embedded `jwk` is the canonical OKP form (member
/// order is free, so the canonical one is reused for determinism);
/// `nonce` and `url` are JSON-escaped so an unusual field cannot break
/// the frame.
///
/// ```wolf-doc-example
/// let pk = "a 32-byte ed25519 public key !!!".bytes()
/// jose.acme_protected_jwk(pk, "aXk6", "https://acme.test/new-account") == "{{\"alg\":\"EdDSA\",\"jwk\":{{\"crv\":\"Ed25519\",\"kty\":\"OKP\",\"x\":\"YSAzMi1ieXRlIGVkMjU1MTkgcHVibGljIGtleSAhISE\"}},\"nonce\":\"aXk6\",\"url\":\"https://acme.test/new-account\"}}"
/// ```
pub fn acme_protected_jwk(pubkey: List[int], nonce: str, url: str) -> str {
    let jwk = jwk_thumbprint_input(pubkey)
    let n = esc(nonce)
    let u = esc(url)
    "{{\"alg\":\"EdDSA\",\"jwk\":{jwk},\"nonce\":\"{n}\",\"url\":\"{u}\"}}"
}

/// The ACME protected header for a request that names the account by URL
/// (RFC 8555 §6.2, every request after newAccount):
/// `{"alg":"EdDSA","kid":"<kid>","nonce":"<nonce>","url":"<url>"}`. `kid`
/// is the account URL the server returned; `nonce` and `url` as above.
/// All three are JSON-escaped.
///
/// ```wolf-doc-example
/// jose.acme_protected_kid("https://acme.test/acct/1", "aXk6", "https://acme.test/new-order") == "{{\"alg\":\"EdDSA\",\"kid\":\"https://acme.test/acct/1\",\"nonce\":\"aXk6\",\"url\":\"https://acme.test/new-order\"}}"
/// ```
pub fn acme_protected_kid(kid: str, nonce: str, url: str) -> str {
    let k = esc(kid)
    let n = esc(nonce)
    let u = esc(url)
    "{{\"alg\":\"EdDSA\",\"kid\":\"{k}\",\"nonce\":\"{n}\",\"url\":\"{u}\"}}"
}

/// The signed ACME request body (RFC 8555 §6.2): the flattened-JSON JWS
/// over `protected` and `payload`, signed with the account key. This is
/// `jws_flattened` under its ACME name — the door ws06 calls after
/// building the header with `acme_protected_jwk`/`acme_protected_kid`. A
/// POST-as-GET (RFC 8555 §6.3) passes an EMPTY `payload` (`List[int]()`),
/// whose base64url is the empty string.
///
/// ```wolf-doc-example
/// let sk = "a 32-byte ed25519 secret key !!!".bytes()
/// let header = jose.acme_protected_kid("https://acme.test/acct/1", "aXk6", "https://acme.test/new-order")
/// let body = jose.acme_body(sk, header, List[int]())
/// body.contains("\"payload\":\"\"")
/// ```
pub fn acme_body(secret: List[int], protected: str, payload: List[int]) -> str {
    jws_flattened(secret, protected, payload)
}
